    {
      "t": "h3",
      "md": "Reading the map as three separate trails"
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "md": "**The data trail.** Everything in stage 1 is a matrix or a count. A hypergraph is written as an incidence matrix $H$ whose entry $H(v,e) = 1$ means vertex $v$ belongs to hyperedge $e$; from it come the two degree matrices $D_v$ and $D_e$, and Equation (1)'s whole propagation operator is built from those three objects plus nothing else. The extension sections for graph theory, linear algebra and the hypergraph family exist because this trail cannot be entered halfway — a reader who is unsure what $H^{\\mathsf{T}}$ does will not be able to follow a single equation afterwards."
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "md": "**The knowledge trail.** Stage 2 is the paper's contribution and the site's longest stretch. Two encoders run in parallel on the same vertex: one counts how the structure groups it, the other walks a pre-trained tree ensemble and records which questions it satisfied and how deep those questions sat. Neither is exotic — the first is a degree with a learnable scaler, the second is a ledger of bits — and the reason they take eight cards to teach is that each has to be built from its prerequisites, which for the second means trees, then boosting, then depth."
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "md": "**The critique trail.** The bottom band is short and deliberately so: four findings out of fourteen are marked as changing what a reader would conclude, and the other ten are collected in card 112 for anyone who wants the full ledger. The map puts them last not because they are least important but because they are unreadable without the rest — a sign error in a delta column means nothing until you know what the delta column measures."
    }
  ],
  "close": "**Next:** the number index — every quantity this site verified, with the card that verified it."
